@Andrzej

Yeah, me, too.  That was one of the places I felt unsteady.  In fact most of my flyspecking was there.  

I'm lucky I found it at all.

@Stephen
I had Whip for 14 down too - but what saved me was the clue for 18 across, which started with the word 'American...'. This led me to think that the answer for 18 across must start with 'US'...

If I hadn't realized that, this corner would have definitely whupped me.

@Andrzej

The Dems are left of the GOP the same way center field is left of right field.

Unless you count AOC, Mamdani, etc...

@Dan

Yes, I was thinking of that. It's sung by Baby's sister (and others) as the final farewell song at Kellerman's.

Wikipedia says that this tune is the basis for many college anthems.

@Andrzej I completely agree with everything you said, including the amount of time it took you, and your comment about if you see someone saying this was too easy 🤣 ...

I started out doing all the across entries, and I got so few of them on the first pass that I wondered if today was going to be a complete fiasco.  I was delighted as the answers slowly started revealing themselves as I went through the down entries, and revisiting the across ones.  So many aha! moments, and wonderful word play.  I also made it through on my own and am very proud of myself.  But I am entirely in awe of the masterful clues!  Kudos to Katie Hoody!

On top of that, what really got me is what she wrote in the constructor notes--her beautiful love letter about and to her Uncle Tim.  As I was reading, I was so moved -- and was getting worried that at the end she was going to say he passed away.  So relieved when she didn't, and she ended with such tender sentiments and even more word play.  You show us how it's done,  Katie.  Wow!

@jennie
I hope Andrzej will follow your advice.
I loved the constructor note (which I seldom if ever read) and it reminded me of the uncle (Dad's favorite brother) who was very kind and attentive to me.  It was tragic that he did not have children (being legally tied to a wife who was institutionalized, sadly) and it was one of his joys to be with our family when possible.  (We were often moving, being Army.)

