@Billy
This is called beating a dead HORSERACE. 
The clue was fine.

@Billy
There’s a difference between a misdirection and a factual error. 
“Track event” = “Event on a track” is not factually incorrect. Similarly, “track meet” is a valid misdirection for “meeting on a track.” Including a “?” would make it too obvious for a Friday or Saturday puzzle. If this is a deal breaker for you, then avoid doing NYT puzzles on those days. Because, I assure you, it is common practice.

@Bill Prada

Ignore my last comment. I hadn't had my glass of coffee yet. :) 

However, if you want to get really technical Websters defines "cupful" as "as much as a cup will hold" or a pint. I think people drink 8 oz of OJ quite often.

@Alexandra Dixon

I love those 'Sleight of hand' clues.  I love it when the harder puzzles are more 'Sleight of hand' and less proper nouns and trivia.

I didn't know about the Ohio river quote either, but in that case.  A four letter word _H__ that's a river.   What else could it be?

@John

Obviously I got OHIO, eventually, because I solved the entire puzzle, but when there are a lot of clues to which I don't know the answers, and they cross each other, it makes bootstrapping the solve more difficult, and this happened all over this puzzle, in every section.  Also, since Jefferson is quoted as saying the river in question is the most beautiful on *earth* and since he did travel abroad, I didn't necessarily assume that it was in the US. 

Just because you made a lucky assumption *and* you know more about US rivers than I do, doesn't mean that your train of thought was inevitable for others.

